%T A245415 1434150,3585375,8963440,22408600,56021500,140053750,350134375,
%U A245415 875335935,2188339840,5470849600,13677124000,34192810000,85482025000,213705062500,534262656250
%N A245415 Number of nonnegative integers with property that their base 5/2 expansion (see A024632) has n digits.
%C A245415 From _Jianing Song_, Oct 15 2026: (Start)
%C A245415 In general, let {f_n}_{n>=0} be the sequence defined by f_{n+1} = alpha*f_n + e_n, where alpha > 1, a <= e_n <= b, then f_n = (f_0 + e_0/alpha + ... + e_{n-1}/alpha^n)*alpha^n = c*alpha^n - (e_n/alpha + e_{n+1}/alpha^2 + ...), where c = f_0 + Sum_{n>=0} e_n/alpha^{n+1}. As a result, we have f_n - f_{n-1} = c*(alpha^n - alpha^(n-1)) + e_{n-1}/alpha - (e_n/alpha + e_{n+1}/alpha^2 + ...)*(1 - 1/alpha). We conclude that c*(alpha^n - alpha^(n-1)) - (b-a)/alpha <= f_n - f_{n-1} <= c*alpha^n + (b-a)/alpha.
%C A245415 Let f_n = A401100(n), with alpha = 5/2, a = 0, and b = 1/2, then we see that (3/5)*(A401103)*(5/2)^n - 1/5 < a(n+1)/5 < (3/5)*(A401103)*(5/2)^n + 1/5 for n >= 1, since we have neither e_n = 0 for all sufficiently large n nor e_n = 1/2 for all sufficiently large n. (End)
%F A245415 a(n) = 5*(A401100(n-1) - A401100(n-2)) for n > 1. - _Jianing Song_, Oct 14 2026
%e A245415 a(2) = 10 because  20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 40, 41, 42, 43 and 44 are the base 5/2 expansions for the integers 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 and 14 respectively and these are the only integers with 2 digits.
%o A245415 (SageMath)
%o A245415 A=[1]
%o A245415 for i in [1..60]:
%o A245415     A.append(ceil(((5-2)/2)*sum(A)))
%o A245415 [5*x for x in A]
%Y A245415 Cf. A081848, A245356, A024632.
%K A245415 nonn,base,changed
%O A245415 1,1
%A A245415 _Hailey R. Olafson_, Jul 21 2014
